; Tutorial_5_Choose_Where_Results_Go.inix -- a spoken walk through BatCon, written for buildTutorials (the format is
; described at the top of Tutorial_0_Overview.inix in the Homer kit's Templates).

[about]
Title=5 - Choose Where Results Go
Intro=Results in a folder of their own, the folder made if needed, and opened when the batch ends.
Setup=BatCon is open with some source files named.
Homework=Send a batch to a new folder and let View output directory open it.

[step]
Say=By default, each converted file goes beside its source. That keeps a file and its conversion together, but a batch of fifty conversions can crowd a folder.

[step]
Say=Alt plus O, Oscar, is Output directory. It names the source's folder until you choose another.
Key=Alt+O
Hear=Output directory: edit, C colon backslash Reports

[step]
Say=To choose a folder by browsing, press Alt plus C, Charlie, for Choose output directory. The standard Browse for Folder dialog opens.
Key=Alt+C
Hear=Choose the output directory

[step]
Say=Arrow keys move through the folders, and Right Arrow opens one to show those inside it. Enter chooses the folder you are on.
Key=Enter
Hear=Output directory: edit, C colon backslash Reports backslash Converted

[step]
Say=You can also type a folder that does not exist yet, such as C colon backslash Reports backslash Markdown. When you press OK, BatCon offers to make it.
Key=Enter
Hear=Create C colon backslash Reports backslash Markdown?
Hear=Yes button

[step]
Say=Yes is the usual answer, so Enter makes the folder and the batch goes on. No returns you to the dialog with nothing changed.
Key=Enter
Hear=Converting 3 files to md

[step]
Say=Once you choose an output folder, it stays put even if you change Source paths; only a folder that still mirrors the source moves with it.

[step]
Say=Two sources with the same name never overwrite each other's result. The second becomes its name followed by underscore 2, the third underscore 3, and so on.

[step]
Say=To open the output folder when a batch ends, check View output directory: Alt plus V, Victor.
Key=Alt+V
Hear=View output directory, check box, checked

[step]
Say=With View output directory checked, File Explorer opens on the output folder after the Results box, with the new files there.
Hear=Markdown

[step]
Say=Use configuration, Alt plus U, keeps the output folder and the check boxes for next time, so a folder you send every batch to need only be chosen once.
Key=Alt+U
Hear=Use configuration, check box, checked

[step]
Say=Default settings puts it all back: Source paths at Documents, Output directory following it, and every box as it was the first time. It is the button after the check boxes, Alt plus D.

[step]
Say=Blank is also a choice. Clear Output directory, and each result goes beside its own source, even when the sources are in many folders.
Key=Alt+O
Hear=Output directory: edit, blank

[step]
Say=Choose output directory opens where Output directory already points, so a folder near the one you want is only a few arrow keys away.
Key=Alt+C
Hear=Choose the output directory

[step]
Say=Escape closes the Browse for Folder dialog without changing anything, and returns you to Output directory.
Key=Escape
Hear=Output directory: edit, C colon backslash Reports

[step]
Say=What this walk taught: Alt plus O and Alt plus C for the output, that BatCon makes a missing folder when you say yes, and Alt plus V and Alt plus L for after the batch.
